-- title:
--   Function field of a Čerednik–Drinfeld quotient as Γ'-invariant meromorphic functions
-- statement:
--   Arithmetic data. Fix a prime $r$ and a characteristic-zero domain $\mathcal O$ which is a discrete valuation ring (`hdvr`) with an irreducible element $\pi$ (`hπ`), complete for the $\pi$-adic topology (`hcomplete`), residue ring of cardinality $r$ (`hres`) and absolutely unramified in the sense that $(r) = (\pi)$ in $\mathcal O$ (`hunr`); let $K_0$ be a characteristic-zero fraction field of $\mathcal O$. Let $Onr$ be a characteristic-zero domain over $\mathcal O$ together with an $\mathcal O$-algebra automorphism $\mathrm{Fr}$, subject to the group of hypotheses describing a completed maximal unramified extension: $Onr$ is complete for the ideal generated by the image of $\pi$ (`hOnr_complete`), that ideal is maximal (`hOnr_max`), every element of $Onr$ satisfies a monic polynomial over $\mathcal O$ modulo that ideal (`hOnr_alg`), every monic polynomial over $Onr$ of positive degree has a root modulo that ideal (`hOnr_closed`), and $\mathrm{Fr}(x)\equiv x^{r}$ modulo that ideal for all $x$ (`hFr`). Let $v\!\det : \mathrm{GL}_2(K_0)\to\mathbb Z$ (written multiplicatively) be a homomorphism characterised by `hvdet`: $v\!\det(g)=n$ if and only if $\det g = u\,\pi^{n}$ in $K_0$ for some unit $u$ of $\mathcal O$.
--
--   Group data. Let $G$ be a group with a homomorphism $\sigma : G\to \mathrm{GL}_2(K_0)$ and a subgroup $\Gamma$ such that some $z\in\Gamma$ has $\sigma z$ a scalar matrix $c\cdot 1$ with $v\!\det(\sigma z)=2$ (`hcent`) and some $w\in\Gamma$ has $v\!\det(\sigma w)=1$ (`hodd`); let $\Gamma'$ be the subgroup characterised by `hΓ'` as consisting of the $x\in\Gamma$ with $v\!\det(\sigma x)$ even. Let $\rho : G\to \mathrm{PGL}_2(K_0)$ be the composite of $\sigma$ with the projection (`hρ`). Three hypotheses concern the action of the image $\rho(\Gamma')$ on the set of vertices [`LT.LatticeTree.Vertex 𝒪 K₀`](def/LatticeTreeOrbital.html#L349), that is on homothety classes of full $\mathcal O$-lattices in $K_0^{2}$: virtual torsion-freeness (`hvtf`), namely the existence of a subgroup $\Gamma''\le\Gamma'$ of non-zero relative index in $\Gamma'$ all of whose elements of finite order in $\rho(\Gamma'')$ are trivial; discreteness (`hdisc`), namely finiteness of $\{g\in\rho(\Gamma') : g\cdot v = v\}$ for every vertex $v$; and cocompactness (`hcocpt`), namely the existence of a finite set $S$ of vertices meeting every $\rho(\Gamma')$-orbit.
--
--   The model and its moduli description. Let $f : \mathcal X\to\operatorname{Spec}\mathcal O$ be proper and flat, with generic fibre (the second projection of the pullback of $f$ along $\operatorname{Spec} K_0\to\operatorname{Spec}\mathcal O$) smooth of relative dimension $1$ (`hsmooth`) and geometrically connected (`hgconn`). Write `Scheme.nilpPoints f` for the functor sending an $\mathcal O$-algebra $B$ with $\pi$ nilpotent to the set of morphisms $\operatorname{Spec}B\to\mathcal X$ over $\operatorname{Spec}\mathcal O$, and `AlgFunctor.prod (AlgFunctor.corep Onr) (Omega K₀ π)` for the functor sending $B$ to the pairs consisting of an $\mathcal O$-algebra map $Onr\to B$ and a Deligne datum over $B$ (a family of submodules `line M` of the base change to $B$ of each full lattice $M$, with invertible quotient, monotone in $M$, equivariant for scalar homotheties, and non-degenerate over every prime of $B$). Let $\Theta$ be a family of maps from the second functor to the first, subject to four hypotheses: naturality in $B$ (`hΘnat`); invariance (`hΘinv`) under the twisted action, i.e. $\Theta$ takes equal values on $x$ and $x'$ whenever `OmegaNr.IsTwistedAct π Onr Fr vdet B (σ γ) x x'` holds for some $\gamma\in\Gamma$, that relation saying that $x'$ has $Onr$-component $x$'s component precomposed with $\mathrm{Fr}^{-v\det(\sigma\gamma)}$ and that the Deligne datum of $x'$ is the pullback of that of $x$ along $(\sigma\gamma)^{-1}$; a fibrewise description (`hΘfib`) which asserts, for every algebraically closed field $k$ over $\mathcal O$ in which $\pi$ is nilpotent and every $\psi : Onr\to_{\mathcal O} k$, that every point of `(Scheme.nilpPoints f).obj k` is of the form $\Theta\,k\,(\psi,P)$ and that $\Theta\,k\,(\psi,P)=\Theta\,k\,(\psi,P')$ holds exactly when $(\psi,P)$ and $(\psi,P')$ are related by the $\sigma\gamma$-twisted action for some $\gamma\in\Gamma$; and universality (`hΘuniv`), which asserts that for every $t : T\to\operatorname{Spec}\mathcal O$ and every natural, $\Gamma$-twist-invariant family $\rho'$ into `Scheme.nilpPoints t` there is a natural family $u$ on `Scheme.nilpPoints f` with $u\circ\Theta=\rho'$, and that any other natural family with the same property agrees with $u$.
--
--   The level family. Let $\iota$ be an index type, and for each $i\in\iota$ let $f_{Y,i} : \mathcal Y_i\to\operatorname{Spec}\mathcal O$ be proper and flat with generic fibre smooth of relative dimension $1$ (`hsmoothY`) and geometrically connected (`hgconnY`), let $\Gamma_{Y,i}\le G$ satisfy the same central and odd-element conditions (`hcentY`, `hoddY`), let $\Gamma'_{Y,i}$ be its subgroup of elements with even $v\!\det\circ\sigma$ (`hΓY'`), with discreteness (`hdiscY`), cocompactness (`hcocptY`) and virtual torsion-freeness (`hvtfY`) for $\rho(\Gamma'_{Y,i})$, and let $\Theta_{Y,i}$ be families satisfying the four analogues of the above for $f_{Y,i}$ and $\Gamma_{Y,i}$ (`hΘYnat`, `hΘYinv`, `hΘYfib`, `hΘYuniv`).
--
--   Analytic data. Let $C$ be a field over $K_0$ carrying a valuation with values in a linearly ordered commutative group with zero $\Gamma_0$, such that for $x$ with $v(x)<1$ and $y\neq 0$ some power $v(x)^{n}\le v(y)$ (`hrk`) and such that every non-zero $\varepsilon\in\Gamma_0$ dominates the value of some non-zero element of $C$ (`hval`); tameness is assumed in the form $v\bigl(\#\{g\in\rho(\Gamma') : g\cdot v = v\}\bigr)=1$ for every vertex $v$ (`htame`), and likewise for each $\rho(\Gamma'_{Y,i})$ (`htameY`). Moreover $C$ is complete and algebraically closed, $\mathcal O$ is cut out inside $K_0$ by $v\le 1$ in $C$ (`h𝒪`), $\varpi$ is a pseudo-uniformiser of $C$ over $K_0$ whose underlying element is the image of $\pi$ (`hϖ`) and which is exhausting, in the sense that every point of the Drinfeld upper half-plane $C\setminus K_0$ lies in one of the affinoids `affinoid ϖ n` (`hex`); the ring `Omega.HolRingOf ϖ ρ` of functions on $C\setminus K_0$ holomorphic on each of these affinoids, with its $G$-action through $\rho$, is assumed to be a domain. Finally $\psi_0 : Onr\to C$ is a ring homomorphism with values of valuation at most $1$ (`hψ₀`) extending $\mathcal O\to K_0\to C$ (`hψ₀𝒪`), and $s_C : \operatorname{Spec}C\to\operatorname{Spec}\mathcal O$ is the morphism induced by $\mathcal O\to K_0\to C$ (`hsC`).
--
--   Conclusion. Under these hypotheses the base change $\mathcal X\times_{\operatorname{Spec}\mathcal O}\operatorname{Spec}C$, realised as `Limits.pullback f sC`, is integral, and there is a ring isomorphism
--   $$e : (\mathcal X\times_{\operatorname{Spec}\mathcal O}\operatorname{Spec}C)\text{-function field} \;\xrightarrow{\ \sim\ }\; \bigl(\operatorname{Frac}(\mathrm{HolRingOf}\,\varpi\,\rho)\bigr)^{\Gamma'} ,$$
--   the target being the subfield `Mumford.invariantFieldOf C G (Omega.HolRingOf ϖ ρ) Γ'` of elements $x$ of the fraction field with $\gamma\cdot x = x$ for all $\gamma\in\Gamma'$, with the following five properties.
--
--   First, $e$ is compatible with constants: for every $c\in C$, the image under $e$ of the element of the function field obtained from $c$ by [`AlgebraicCurve.baseToFunctionField`](def/AlgebraicCurve_CurveModel.html#L18) applied to the projection to $\operatorname{Spec}C$ equals the image of $c$ under the structure map of the invariant field.
--
--   Second, $e$ converts correspondences coming from $G$-elements of trivial determinant valuation into the $G$-action: for every endomorphism $a$ of $\mathcal X$ over $\operatorname{Spec}\mathcal O$ and every $g\in G$ with $v\!\det(\sigma g)=1$ such that the natural transformation `Scheme.nilpPoints.mapHom f f a` sends $\Theta\,B\,x$ to $\Theta\,B\,x'$ whenever $x'$ is the $\sigma g$-twisted translate of $x$, and for every endomorphism $a_C$ of the pullback with $a_C$ followed by the first projection equal to the first projection followed by $a$ and $a_C$ followed by the second projection equal to the second projection: for every open $U$ containing the generic point whose $a_C$-preimage also contains it, and every section $s$ over $U$, the image under $e$ of the germ at the generic point of $a_C^{*}s$ equals $g^{-1}\cdot$ (the image under $e$ of the germ of $s$), as elements of $\operatorname{Frac}(\mathrm{HolRingOf}\,\varpi\,\rho)$.
--
--   Third, the same statement for the Frobenius correspondence: for every endomorphism $b$ of $\mathcal X$ over $\operatorname{Spec}\mathcal O$ whose effect on nilpotent points is $\Theta\,B\,x\mapsto \Theta\,B\,(\mathrm{frobTwist}\,\mathrm{Fr}\,1\,x_1, x_2)$, i.e. precomposition of the $Onr$-component with $\mathrm{Fr}$, for every compatible endomorphism $b_C$ of the pullback over $\operatorname{Spec}C$, and for every $w\in\Gamma$ with $v\!\det(\sigma w)=1$: germs transform by $w^{-1}\cdot$ in the same sense as above.
--
--   Fourth, semilinear automorphisms of the coefficient field are matched with integral powers of such a $w$: for every isometric automorphism $s$ of $C$ fixing $K_0$ pointwise and every $n\in\mathbb Z$ such that $s(\psi_0 y)=\psi_0(\mathrm{Fr}^{n}y)$ for all $y\in Onr$ fixed by $\mathrm{Fr}^{2}$, for every endomorphism $t_C$ of the pullback with $t_C$ followed by the first projection equal to the first projection and $t_C$ followed by the second projection equal to the second projection followed by $\operatorname{Spec}(s)$, and for every $w\in\Gamma$ with $v\!\det(\sigma w)=1$: the image under $e$ of the germ of $t_C^{*}(\mathrm{sec})$ equals $w^{n}\cdot$ applied to the image of the germ of $\mathrm{sec}$ under the automorphism of $\operatorname{Frac}(\mathrm{HolRingOf}\,\varpi\,\rho)$ induced by the ambient semilinear automorphism `Omega.toAmbientOf ϖ ρ s`, whose base is $s$ and which acts on holomorphic functions coefficientwise through $s$.
--
--   Fifth, the same description holds at every level of the family, compatibly with degeneracy maps: for each $i\in\iota$ the pullback $\mathcal Y_i\times_{\operatorname{Spec}\mathcal O}\operatorname{Spec}C$ is integral and there is a ring isomorphism $e_{Y}$ from its function field onto the $\Gamma'_{Y,i}$-invariant subfield of $\operatorname{Frac}(\mathrm{HolRingOf}\,\varpi\,\rho)$ which is compatible with constants from $C$ in the same sense as $e$, and such that for every morphism $h : \mathcal Y_i\to\mathcal X$ with $h$ followed by $f$ equal to $f_{Y,i}$ and every $g\in G$ with $v\!\det(\sigma g)=1$ for which `Scheme.nilpPoints.mapHom (fY i) f h` sends $\Theta_{Y,i}\,B\,x$ to $\Theta\,B\,x'$ whenever $x'$ is the $\sigma g$-twisted translate of $x$, and every morphism $h_C$ of pullbacks compatible with $h$ and with the projections to $\operatorname{Spec}C$, the image under $e_Y$ of the germ at the generic point of $h_C^{*}(\mathrm{sec})$ equals $g^{-1}\cdot$ (the image under $e$ of the germ of $\mathrm{sec}$), for every open $U$ containing the relevant generic points and every section $\mathrm{sec}$ over $U$.
--
--   This is the function-field form of the Čerednik–Drinfeld uniformisation: the generic fibre of the integral model $\mathcal X$, based changed to a complete algebraically closed valued field $C$, has function field the field of $\Gamma'$-invariant meromorphic functions on Drinfeld's upper half-plane, and the isomorphism transports correspondences on $\mathcal X$ (Hecke-type endomorphisms, the Frobenius correspondence, semilinear automorphisms of $C$, and degeneracy maps from a family of higher levels) into the action of the corresponding elements of $G$ and of the induced automorphisms of the holomorphic ring. It is used to produce Mumford-type embeddings of the quotient curves in the two `exists_mumfordEmbedding_of_cerednikDrinfeld_uniformization` statements.
